s becoming clear that 80 of  canadians who died in this pandemic were  living in our long-term care homes we  had  all of our provincial and and federal  leaders coming  out saying the system is broken but  fixing it means  billions of dollars of investment  [Music]  welcome back you'd think we would have  learned more by now  yet at some long-term care facilities  across canada there is an alarming rise  in covid19 cases  and deaths it feels like patterns we saw  in the spring are repeating  now what needs to happen to stop this  catastrophic trend  dr samir sinha is one of the leading  authorities on caring for the elderly  he is the director of geriatrics at  sinai health in toronto  we're now in a second wave across canada  there's out-of-control community spread  happening in places like toronto and  manitoba  are care homes better prepared for this  second wave  we haven't seen the same degree of  outbreaks and i think that reflects the  fact that  homes now have access to ppe they now  have staff that are not working between  multiple settings  for example but what really is  concerning is that we're still seeing  that with a lack of staffing and with  with  other issues in terms of the levels of  community spread that these homes  continue to remain vulnerable  and we are certainly seeing a number of  homes across the country that are having  spectacular outbreaks that were  representing that within many homes  there are weaknesses that still exist  that we have been able to rectify  between the first and second waves  is it that the underlying issue in  society and the health care system  doesn't value residents in long-term  care because they are seen as being  close to death well i think this is a  huge issue because when you actually say  well why did long-term care  homes in canada why were they so  devastated compared to other countries  well the fact is when we compare  ourselves to other rich industrialized  countries  canada spends about a third less on its  long-term care systems  than other countries do so because we  spend a lot less  right off the bat it means that we have  less staffing  available in our homes to deliver the  care that's actually needed  and we also have poor quality facilities  both of these were major factors  that set our homes up to fail i think  the other thing that concerns me is that  we have manitoba for example that's  currently going through its first wave  if you will  and they actually had the opportunity to  witness what happened in the rest of  canada  but what really pains me is actually to  see that  you know they're saying well hindsight's  20 20 and we didn't realize we could get  this bad or these outbreaks could occur  in such a significant way and it's  almost like they didn't actually  really learn the lessons that the rest  of us learned the hard way back in the  spring there was a long delay i think  about three weeks between when bc  ordered personal support workers must  only work in one home  and when that same order was given in  ontario it seemed so basic in hindsight  why do you think it didn't happen right  across the country right away  what we do know is that in a fast-moving  pandemic  you know speed actually matters and  making decisions  in a timely way will actually save lives  so i don't know what the disconnect is  and why so much delays have actually  taken place  in implementing clear science based  evidence but what we do know is that the  provinces that continue to delay  implementing the right  policies and recommendations are seeing  way more deaths that are absolutely  preventable  ontario premier doug ford has said he is  genuinely heartbroken biola deaths in  long-term care and  we know his own mother-in-law lives in a  care home and she caught covid19  it's not that governments and  politicians don't get it so  why has the system failed people so  badly  you know the challenge was that when it  was becoming clear that 80 percent of  canadians who died in this pandemic were  living in our long-term care homes we  had all of our  provincial and and federal leaders  coming out saying the system is broken  we're going to fix it and we'll do what  it takes but fixing it means  billions of dollars of investment  because right now in ontario  we're providing the average resident  with about 2.7 hours of care every day  when our own government's expert  advisors say it should be four hours  so this is where we then need to put an  investment in but governments have been  dragging their feet  and we just saw that in the recent  ontario budget right before the budget  announcement they made a deliberate  point  to actually say we are going to meet  that standard of four hours of care a  day  which would come with a 1.6 billion  dollar price tag  but they said it would come by 2024.25  and then i think what really upset  families and and many advocates like  myself  was to see that in the budget that came  out a few days later  there was no money to be seen even  though the premier reiterated  that we are committed and that we will  find the money when it's necessary  well it's necessary now  [Music]  next week on the new reality we've  never ever planned in halton here to  have a cn  intermodal uh facility in this community  with trucks like in terms like the  exhaust streams they're gonna be  bringing like  all like the carcinogens and things like  that they're probably gonna be bringing  in and i don't want to be breathing that  in i don't want my kids breathing it in  you can't have you know kids on their  tricycles on their bikes  families driving with 15 1600 trucks on  the road this can happen anywhere across  canada and it shouldn't be allowed it's  it's not right  it's the wrong terminal in the wrong  place  at the wrong time and i just feel that  cn is part of the expression  railroading this one through  [Music]  all that and more next saturday on the  new reality  i'm donna friesen thanks for watchin